<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pancho Villa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1878">1878</a>–<a href="/years/1923">1923</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Francisco Villa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pancho Villa was a Mexican revolutionary general from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> who led the Division of the North in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-revolution">Mexican Revolution</a>. He helped overthrow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victoriano-huerta">Victoriano Huerta</a> in 1914, but after breaking with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venustiano-carranza">Venustiano Carranza</a> he was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvaro-obregon">Álvaro Obregón</a> at Celaya in 1915. His raid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>, in 1916 provoked the American Punitive Expedition, and he was assassinated in 1923.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pancho-villa.jpg" alt="Pancho Villa" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: D.W. Hoffman, an El Paso photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General_Francisco_Villa_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
